      "## Definition\n\nA **real kernel** on a set $X$ is a function $k:X\\times X\\to\\mathbb R$. It is **positive semidefinite** if it is symmetric and, for every integer $n\\ge1$, every $x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\in X$, and every $c_1,\\ldots,c_n\\in\\mathbb R$,\n\n$$\\sum_{i=1}^n\\sum_{j=1}^n c_i c_j k(x_i,x_j)\\ge0.$$\n\nA **real reproducing kernel Hilbert space with kernel $k$** is a real Hilbert space $\\mathcal H$ of functions $X\\to\\mathbb R$ such that, for each $x\\in X$, the section $k_x:=k(\\cdot,x)$ lies in $\\mathcal H$ and $\\langle f,k_x\\rangle_{\\mathcal H}=f(x)$ for every $f\\in\\mathcal H$. Consequently $\\langle k_z,k_x\\rangle_{\\mathcal H}=k(z,x)=k(x,z)$.\n\nFor the canonical construction, let $E_0$ be the real vector space of finitely supported functions $a:X\\to\\mathbb R$, write $\\delta_x$ for the function supported at $x$ with value $1$, and put\n\n$$B(a,b):=\\sum_{x\\in\\operatorname{supp}(a)}\\sum_{z\\in\\operatorname{supp}(b)}a(x)b(z)k(x,z).$$\n\nLet $N:=\\{a\\in E_0:B(a,a)=0\\}$. The canonical space is formed by giving $E_0/N$ its induced inner product and taking a norm completion; the image of $\\delta_x$ is denoted $k_x$. For an element $h$ of that completion, its associated function is $x\\mapsto\\langle h,k_x\\rangle$. The quotient, completed inner product, and function identification are justified by the local construction theorem."
    ],
    "lem-finite-dimensional-projection-in-a-real-inner-product-space": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $E$ be a real inner-product space, let $M\\subseteq E$ be a finite-dimensional subspace, and let $f\\in E$. There is a unique $p\\in M$ such that $f-p$ is orthogonal to every vector of $M$. This vector is the unique nearest point in $M$ to $f$, and it satisfies $\\|p\\|\\le\\|f\\|$. The conclusion does not require $E$ to be complete."
    ],
    "thm-canonical-rkhs-construction-and-reproducing-property": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Countable Choice. For every set $X$ and every real positive semidefinite kernel $k$ on $X$, the canonical construction from finitely supported coefficients, quotient by zero seminorm, and norm completion defines a real reproducing kernel Hilbert space $\\mathcal H_k$. For every $x\\in X$, its kernel section $k_x=k(\\cdot,x)$ belongs to $\\mathcal H_k$ and satisfies $\\langle h,k_x\\rangle_{\\mathcal H_k}=h(x)$ for all $h\\in\\mathcal H_k$. The choice assumption here is countable choice; this statement does not assert the same construction in ZF without that assumption."
    ],
    "lem-positive-semidefinite-gram-matrix-has-euclidean-feature-factorization": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $k$ be a real positive semidefinite kernel on a set $X$, let $n\\ge1$, and let $x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\in X$. There are vectors $z_1,\\ldots,z_n\\in\\mathbb R^n$ such that $k(x_i,x_j)=z_i^Tz_j$ for every $1\\le i,j\\le n$. The vectors may be zero and need not be linearly independent."
    ],
    "thm-finite-sample-representer-theorem-in-an-rkhs": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\mathcal H$ be a real reproducing kernel Hilbert space with kernel $k$, let $n\\ge0$, let $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$ be sample points, let $L:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ be finite-valued, and let $\\Omega:[0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ be nondecreasing. Define $J(f):=L(f(x_1),\\ldots,f(x_n))+\\Omega(\\|f\\|_{\\mathcal H})$. If $J$ has a minimizer, then it has a minimizer in $\\operatorname{span}\\{k(\\cdot,x_i):1\\le i\\le n\\}$; equivalently, at least one minimizer has the finite expansion $f(\\cdot)=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\alpha_i k(\\cdot,x_i)$. This is an existence-conditional statement and asserts no minimizer exists when none is separately guaranteed."
    ],
    "def-hard-and-soft-margin-support-vector-machines": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $X$ be a set, let $\\mathcal H$ be a real Hilbert space, and let $\\phi:X\\to\\mathcal H$ be a feature map. Define its kernel by $k(x,z):=\\langle\\phi(x),\\phi(z)\\rangle_{\\mathcal H}$. For a finite labeled sample $(x_i,y_i)_{i=1}^n$ with $n\\ge1$, $x_i\\in X$, and $y_i\\in\\{-1,+1\\}$, the **soft-margin support-vector machine** with cost $C>0$ is\n\n$$\\min_{w\\in\\mathcal H,\\ b\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\xi\\in\\mathbb R^n}\\ \\frac12\\|w\\|_{\\mathcal H}^2+C\\sum_{i=1}^n\\xi_i$$\n\nsubject to\n\n$$y_i\\bigl(\\langle w,\\phi(x_i)\\rangle_{\\mathcal H}+b\\bigr)\\ge1-\\xi_i,\\qquad \\xi_i\\ge0\\quad(1\\le i\\le n).$$\n\nThe bias $b$ is unpenalized. The associated real score is $s_{w,b}(x):=\\langle w,\\phi(x)\\rangle_{\\mathcal H}+b$. If a binary label is required, use the deterministic rule $+1$ when $s_{w,b}(x)\\ge0$ and $-1$ otherwise. The score is not defined as a probability, and this optimization includes no probability-calibration map.\n\nThe **hard-margin** problem removes the slack variables and minimizes $\\frac12\\|w\\|_{\\mathcal H}^2$ subject to $y_i(\\langle w,\\phi(x_i)\\rangle_{\\mathcal H}+b)\\ge1$ for every $i$. In this library, “margin-separable sample” means exactly that this hard-margin feasible set is nonempty; no hard-margin conclusion is asserted for a sample that fails this condition."
    ],
    "lem-soft-margin-svm-primal-attainment": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $n\\ge1$, let $C>0$, and let $(x_i,y_i)_{i=1}^n$ be a finite labeled sample with $y_i\\in\\{-1,+1\\}$ in a real Hilbert feature space $\\mathcal H$, with feature map $\\phi$. The soft-margin SVM primal has a finite attained minimum. At least one minimizing tuple has its weight vector in $M:=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\phi(x_i):1\\le i\\le n\\}$. This conclusion includes samples with only one label and does not assert uniqueness of the bias or slack variables."
    ],
    "thm-soft-margin-svm-dual-and-kkt-conditions": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $n\\ge1$, $C>0$, let $\\mathcal H$ be a real Hilbert space, and let $\\phi:X\\to\\mathcal H$ be a feature map with kernel $k(x,z)=\\langle\\phi(x),\\phi(z)\\rangle_{\\mathcal H}$. For any finite sample $(x_i,y_i)_{i=1}^n$ with $y_i\\in\\{-1,+1\\}$, the soft-margin SVM primal attains its minimum. Its Lagrangian dual is\n\n$$\\max_{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R^n}\\ \\sum_{i=1}^n\\alpha_i-\\frac12\\sum_{i=1}^n\\sum_{j=1}^n\\alpha_i\\alpha_jy_iy_jk(x_i,x_j)$$\n\nsubject to $0\\le\\alpha_i\\le C$ for every $i$ and $\\sum_i\\alpha_i y_i=0$. The dual maximum is attained and equals the primal minimum. For every primal minimizer there are dual multipliers $\\alpha_i$ and $\\mu_i$ such that\n\n$$w=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\alpha_i y_i\\phi(x_i),\\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^n\\alpha_i y_i=0,\\qquad C-\\alpha_i-\\mu_i=0,$$\n\n$$\\alpha_i\\bigl(1-\\xi_i-y_i(\\langle w,\\phi(x_i)\\rangle+b)\\bigr)=0,\\qquad \\mu_i\\xi_i=0,$$\n\nwith $\\alpha_i\\ge0$ and $\\mu_i\\ge0$. Conversely, any primal-feasible tuple and nonnegative multipliers satisfying these stationarity and complementarity equations are globally optimal. An index with $\\alpha_i>0$ is a support vector; if $0<\\alpha_i<C$, then $\\xi_i=0$ and its signed margin equals $1$. The single dual equality row is independent because $(y_1,\\ldots,y_n)\\ne0$."
    ],
